Macau — Understand

Understand
As the first and last European colony in East Asia, Macau has more visible colonial history than Hong Kong. The buildings and cobblestone or patterned brick streets in many parts of the city, particularly the center of the old city and Coloane, look much like somewhere in Mediterranean Europe. However, the people and the Chinese-language signage indicate Asia. The Portuguese and Macanese population continues to maintain a presence, but most of the population is ethnically Chinese.
Like neighbouring Hong Kong, Macau operates under a "One Country, Two Systems" principle, retaining most laws and governing structures from colonial times. Although part of China, Macau has its own border control, currency, legal system and postal service, so for most tourists it feels like visiting a different country.
Macau is unique in many ways, but it also has many close cultural and economic ties with Hong Kong and the Pearl River Delta region across the border.
Geography
How small is Macau?: As of 2024, the total land area of Macau is . This is equivalent to:
- 68 times the size of Vatican City;
- 16 times the size of Monaco;
- 11 times the size of City of London;
- 80% the size of Shanghai Pudong International Airport;
- one-third the size of Lisbon proper;
- 4.5% the size of Singapore;
- 3% the size of Hong Kong;
- 2% the size of Zhuhai;
- 0.2% the size of Beijing.
And as a side note, around half of Macau's land mass was obtained by reclamation.
Besides the city (Macau/Peninsula), Macau includes the islands of Taipa and Coloane, which are connected to the peninsula by bridges and to each other by a causeway. The area between the two islands has been reclaimed from the sea and built up into the Cotai Strip; that has become an area of intense development with many new casinos and hotels.
The mainland Chinese city of Zhuhai borders Macau to the north and west, and the border crossing carries heavy two-way vehicular and pedestrian traffic. The Zhuhai Special Economic Zone extends south to Hengqin Island, an area west of Taipa, Cotai and Coloane; the Lotus Bridge from Cotai connects to that area.
